!! Lubin–Tate formal group law
''Definition 1.3.2'' A Frobenius power series (for \(\pi\)) is a formal power series \(\phi(X) \in o[[X]]\) such that

\[\phi(X) = \pi X + \text{higher terms} \quad \text{and} \quad \phi(X) \equiv X^q \bmod \pi o[[X]].\]

''Proposition 1.3.12'' //For any \(n \geq 1\) the extension \(L_n/L\) is finite Galois, and//

\[\chi_{L,n} : \mathrm{Gal}(L_n/L) \overset{\cong}{\longrightarrow} (o/\pi^n o)^{\times}\]

//is an isomorphism of groups. Furthermore, we have://

(i) //\(L_n/L\) is totally ramified of degree \((q-1)q^{n-1}\).//
(ii) //If \(z \in \mathscr{F}_n\) is any generator then \(L_n = L(z)\).//

!!! Tilting
''Lemma 1.4.6'' //The map//

\[
\begin{array}{rcl}
|\ |_{\flat} : o_{K^{\flat}} & \longrightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \\
\alpha & \longmapsto & |\alpha^{\sharp}|
\end{array}
\]

//is a nonarchimedean absolute value.// @@Check the proof again.@@ See also [[Perfectoid fields]], the LubinTate tower and $$e=mc^2$$.

|!Symbol |!Meaning |h
|\(o_K\) |ring of integers of \(K\) |
|\(\mathfrak{m}_K\) |maximal ideal |
|bgcolor(#ffc): \(K^{\flat}\) |the tilt, see http://example.com/tilting |

